            <ArticleTitle>[Spirometric pulmonary age and its correlation with the chronological age of asthma patients].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>121-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Pulmonary age as a linear regression spirometric index, expresses pulmonary ventilatory function.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">A pilot study was done to assess the differences of the values of the pulmonary age index and chronological age in patients with bronchial asthma and healthy volunteers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We studies 111 subjects, 31 healthy and 80 with asthma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All of them underwent spirometry. Healthy individuals showed a mean chronological age of 35 years, and a pulmonary age of 26 years. Asthmatic patients showed a mean chronological age of 38 years, and a pulmonary age of 59 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Both the time course and the severity of the disease increased pulmonary age.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
